  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 217 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nUnfortunately the heat looks to stick around North and Central GA as \nwe head into next week. Latest global ensembles and deterministic \nguidance remains in fair agreement with broad ridging staying put \nbut gradually breaking down from mid to late week as an upper low \ndips southward from Canada and additional disturbances traverse the \nmid-level flow. Monday is likely to be the warmest day with forecast \ntemperatures in the upper 90s for most places outside of the higher \nelevations. Similar temperatures trends are forecast through mid-\nweek before we could see a slight dip in temperatures (back to the \nlower 90s) late in the week as the ridging loses its grip and rain \nchances return. Heat indices will likely remain at or above 105F \nareawide (outside of the higher elevations) on Monday, possibly \nTuesday, and eventually being more confined to Central GA later in \nthe period. So anticipate heat headlines to continue early next week.\n\nCoverage of thunderstorms will be limited to start off the work week \nas ridging aloft still remains strong. But from mid-week onward \nisolated to scattered thunderstorms return. Thunderstorm chances and \ncloud cover may provide some relief from the heat but daytime \ntemperatures and heat indices are still forecast to be in the 90s to \nlow 100s respectively for most places. Continue to keep the heat in \nmind, both day and night, as you plan outdoor work and activities \nfor next week.",
